  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061747/assets/3ec88af84d96caed2c27b9b1596b5320ff7ceaaac71ce12e48623c2be52f18ba.png)\n\nIn **ascending order**, the values of $t$ at which the curve $x=t+6,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-4$ intersects the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-1$ (shown above) are\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,t=$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we expect two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of the intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-4 & =\\frac{1}{2}(t+6)-1 \\\\ {t}^{2}-8 & =(t+6)-2 \\\\ {t}^{2}-8 & =t+4 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t-12 & =0 \\\\ (t+3)(t-4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the values of $t$ where the curve $x=t+6,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-4$ intersects the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-1$ are $t=-3$ and $t=4.$",
